# PROFIL(2)

## NAME

profil - execution time profile

## SYNOPSIS

```c
int profil(char *buff, int bufsiz, int offset, int scale);
```

## DESCRIPTION

**Note: this call is refused here, and everything below describes what it did on v7.** A *scale*
of 0 or 1 succeeds - that is v7's own "profiling off" and it is the only state there is - and any
other *scale* fails with EINVAL. See BESM-6 NOTES; the page is kept as the record of what the call
was for.

*Buff* points to an area of core whose length (in bytes) is given by *bufsiz*. After this call,
the user's program counter (pc) is examined each clock tick. **Note:** v7 said a sixtieth of a
second. HZ is **250** here. *offset* is subtracted from it, and the result multiplied by
*scale*. If the resulting number corresponds to a word inside *buff,* that word is incremented.

The scale is interpreted as an unsigned, fixed-point fraction with binary point at the left:
0177777(8) gives a 1-1 mapping of pc's to words in *buff;* 077777(8) maps each pair of
instruction words together. 02(8) maps all instructions onto the beginning of *buff* (producing
a non-interrupting core clock).

Profiling is turned off by giving a *scale* of 0 or 1. It is rendered ineffective by giving a
*bufsiz* of 0. Profiling is turned off when an *exec* is executed, but remains on in child and
parent both after a *fork*. Profiling may be turned off if an update in *buff* would cause a
memory fault.

## BESM-6 NOTES

**The description above is v7's, and this call does none of it.** *Profil* is refused: a *scale*
of 0 or 1 - which v7 itself reads as "profiling off", and off is the only state this machine has -
returns 0 and does nothing, and **any other scale returns -1 with errno set to EINVAL**. The
*buff*, *bufsiz* and *offset* arguments are not looked at. No word of *buff* is ever incremented,
and there is no clock sampling behind the call at all: the kernel has no *addupc* and carries no
profiling descriptor in the u-area.

The reason is that the other half of the machinery is missing and is not coming. What v7's page
pointed at as monitor(3), and what **cc -p** would install - *monitor* and *mcount* - has not been
ported, this compiler has no **-p**, and *prof*(1) is deliberately not ported either, so nothing
would read a *mon.out* that nothing produces. Accepting the call and silently recording nothing
was the worse of the two answers. Should profiling ever be wanted, those three come first; and
note that v7's kernel side does not transliterate, its buckets being 16-bit shorts indexed by a
*byte* offset through a 16-bit binary fraction, where a buffer here would be **words** and a
bucket index a **word** index.

**Profiling a program on this machine is a simulator job.** *B6sim* counts what it interprets
with no kernel help; it refuses this call too, with EPERM.

## DIAGNOSTICS

Returns 0 for a *scale* of 0 or 1, and -1 with *errno* set to **EINVAL** for any other, this
being a request to sample.

## SEE ALSO

intro(2)

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_profil** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
